          "text": "Alcohol",
          "is_correct": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "ANSWER: C Fetal alcohol syndrome affects about one third of all infants born to alcoholic women. It is characterized by growth retardation, microphthalmia, short palpebral fissures, midface hypoplasia (smooth or short philtrum and a thin upper lip), microcephaly, intellectual deficits, and seizures. The incidence of infants born with fetal alcohol syndrome is about 0.5 per 1,000 live births. Marijuana use in pregnancy is associated with low birth weight, prematurity, and withdrawal symptoms, including excessive crying, tremors, and hyperemesis. Crack cocaine use by the mother causes increased irritability and crying spells in infants. Heroin leads to withdrawal syndrome in infants. Nicotine causes low birth weight and premature births.",
